// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(4): every
// potentially-evaluated call to an immediate function shall produce a
// constant expression. The implicit object argument is part of that call, so
// a *temporary* receiver is admissible exactly when the temporary's own
// initialization is a constant expression -- here a `consteval` constructor
// call, which §9.1(6) permits because `Counter` satisfies
// docs/spec/en/06-constant-evaluation.md (ch06) §7.2(2).
// §7.2(6.2) requires the implementation to support both the constructor call
// and the member call.
//
// Call position: block scope. Callee: `consteval` non-static member function.
// Receiver: temporary.
//
// Expected value derived from the spec text above and independently confirmed
// with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which compiles this exact source and exits 42.
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS. scpp resolves the member call but
// then reports "error: internal error: no generated code for resolved
// function 'Counter_doubled' called here" -- the immediate member call on a
// temporary receiver is lowered as a runtime call instead of being folded.
// The expectation above comes from the spec and from clang; do not weaken it
// to match the implementation.
class Counter {
public:
    virtual ~Counter() = default;
public:
    int value{};

    consteval Counter(int v) {
        this->value = v;
        return;
    }

    consteval int doubled() const {
        return this->value * 2;
    }
};

int main() {
    return Counter{21}.doubled();
}
